{# Every section is a {% block %}, so --prompts reaches all of them; those stating how the server behaves are safest added to rather than replaced. #}
{% block intro %}Panel Live Server executes Python code snippets and renders the resulting visualizations as live, interactive web pages.{% endblock %}

{% block workflow %}WORKFLOW:
`show(code, name, method)` renders a visualization and gives it to the user.
`screenshot(code=...)` renders one and returns the picture to YOU only — nothing
reaches the chat and nothing is added to the user's feed.
So when you want to check your own output before delivering it, screenshot the
draft, fix what is wrong, screenshot again, and finish with `show`.
Each `screenshot(code=...)` reports a draft id. When the draft finally looks
right, call `show(draft_id=...)` with that id rather than `show(code=...)` with
the code pasted again: the draft has already been rendered and checked, so
promoting it runs nothing, sends nothing twice, and gives the user exactly the
version you approved.
`edit(snippet_id, old_str, new_str)` changes part of something already stored,
without you resending it. It works on a draft (edited in place) and on something
the user has already been shown (forked into a new draft, so their version does
not change under them). The edited code is run before it returns, so the id you
get back can go straight to `show(draft_id=...)`; screenshot it first only when
you need to SEE the change rather than just make it. Prefer this over resending
the whole snippet when you are adjusting something small — a colour, a title, one
line — since a full rewrite costs you the entire snippet each round. Resending is
still fine for short snippets or sweeping changes.
Never call `show` on work you are still iterating on, and never call it just to
get a snippet_id to screenshot.
`evaluate(code)` runs code and returns its TEXT output to you — whatever it
printed plus the value of the last expression. No picture, no browser, nothing
reaches the user. Reach for it whenever you want a VALUE rather than an
appearance: check that an option is accepted, inspect a rendered Bokeh model,
confirm a DataFrame's columns. Do not use it to answer how something LOOKS —
rendered output and raw data disagree, and that is what `screenshot` is for.
Static validation (syntax, security, packages) runs in ~50 ms.
The iframe loads immediately via Panel's WebSocket — no prior validate() needed.{% endblock %}

{% block file_policy %}DO NOT write the visualization code to a file, script, notebook, or `examples/`
directory, and do not run it in a separate shell/REPL. Pass the code string
straight to `show(code=...)` — it executes the code for you. Creating files
clutters the user's repository and is not wanted.{% endblock %}

{% block library_selection %}LIBRARY SELECTION:
PRIMARILY write visualizations with HoloViz packages — they integrate best with
the live server and are ALWAYS installed:
- hvPlot: quick interactive plots from DataFrames (.hvplot API)
- HoloViews: advanced composable, interactive visualizations
- Panel: dashboards, data apps, complex layouts (use method='server')
Also always available (no extra package needed):
- Bokeh: HoloViz's default backend, for low-level interactive web plots
- ECharts (pn.pane.ECharts): business-quality charts with transitions/animations, from a spec dict
- deck.gl (pn.pane.DeckGL): large-scale geospatial and 3D visualization, from a spec dict
Other well-known libraries (Matplotlib, Plotly, seaborn, Altair) are installed ONLY
when the optional 'pydata' dependencies are present, so they may be MISSING — prefer
HoloViz. You do NOT need to check availability up front: if an import is not installed,
`show` returns the validation error — read it and rewrite using a HoloViz package.
A non-Bokeh renderer (Plotly, deck.gl, a Terminal pane, Tabulator, ECharts, Altair/Vega,
ipywidgets, VTK, Vizzu) also needs its JS extension declared with `pn.extension("...")`,
e.g. `pn.extension("plotly")` — required for method='server', inferred automatically for
method='inline'. The detector is a SUBSTRING SCAN over your lowercased source, not an
import analysis — the word appearing anywhere (a comment, a string, a variable name)
triggers the requirement. If you get an unexpected [extensions] error, search your
snippet for the word rather than the import.
If a `Skill` (or equivalent skill-search) tool is available, invoke the
`developing-with-holoviz` skill before writing HoloViz code — it covers plot APIs,
widget choice, and dashboard architecture in depth. This prompt only covers how to
execute code through THIS server; it is not a HoloViz reference.{% endblock %}

{% block rendering %}RENDERING
Visualizations are served by the live Panel server, so Panel reactive patterns (pn.bind, @pn.depends, param.watch, .servable()) work everywhere. Use method='server' for any interactive Panel app.
Clients whose iframes cannot reach localhost (Claude Desktop, Cowork) show an 'Open in browser' button instead of an inline preview; the visualization itself is fully interactive once opened. Nothing about the code you write changes.{% endblock %}

{% block output %}OUTPUT
After calling `show`, ALWAYS present the returned URL to the user as a clickable Markdown link: [Show Visualization](url){% endblock %}

{% block errors %}ERRORS
Snippets execute in the user's real Python environment, with their filesystem and
network. `show` raises `SecurityError` for code that deletes or overwrites files, reads
credentials, exfiltrates data, opens network connections, or otherwise has no business
in visualization code, regardless of how the request is framed — a blocklist (pickle,
subprocess, socket, ctypes, importlib, and similar) backstops this but is not the
policy. This requires a substantive code rewrite, not a retry.
`show` raises `ValidationError` for syntax, package, extension, and servable problems —
each message already names the fix, so read and apply it rather than guessing. An
[extensions] error is often a false alarm from the substring scan described under
LIBRARY SELECTION, not a genuinely missing pn.extension(...) call.
Two failures are silent instead of raising anything, so watch for them yourself: with
method='inline', the last line must be a bare expression (not an assignment) or the
page renders an empty "no output to display" box with nothing telling you why; a
snippet that never finishes is likely too slow for the 30s execution timeout —
aggregate or subset the data rather than plotting it raw.{% endblock %}
